resolve is a third consumer of the residue format git-span/conflict-residue-boundary declares, and the only one that must reconstruct the anchor/why boundary rather than write it: the format separates the two regions with a blank line and nothing else, so resolve establishes the boundary structurally against classify_anchor_line — the shared authority on whether a line could be an anchor record — and refuses, leaving the file byte-identical, on the shapes that make the two split sides disagree about where the boundary falls. Anchors and why come unconditionally from the live worktree text via --fix's marker-split and clean-source precondition, while unmerged index stages supplement only [config], base for three-way arbitration, and theirs' why in both directions — the writer emits ours_why on its non-diverging branch, so stage 3 is the sole surviving evidence both when theirs added a why the text omits and when theirs deleted one the text still shows. resolve canonicalizes marker length before that split because split_conflict_markers recognizes a separator of exactly seven '=', and it keys its pre-kernel refusals on whether an anchor's source can be read rather than on which side declared it, so it stays in step with the kernel's own view of an anchor rather than with --fix's orphan bookkeeping; it never calls --fix's partial-write or rename-recovery paths.
